      "worked": "The original expression requires $\\sin x\\ne0$ and $\\cos x\\ne0$.\n\nUse $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$, $\\sec^2 x=1+\\tan^2 x$ and $\\operatorname{cosec}^2 x=1+\\cot^2 x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3+2\\tan^2 x+2\\cot^2 x&=7\\\\\\tan^2 x+\\cot^2 x&=2\\end{align*}$$\n\nLet $t=\\tan^2 x>0$. Since $\\cot^2 x=\\frac{1}{t}$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}t+\\frac1t&=2\\\\t^2-2t+1&=0\\\\(t-1)^2&=0\\\\t&=1\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan^2 x&=1\\\\\\tan x&=\\pm1\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe related angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Both signs together give one solution in each quadrant:\n\n$$x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\ \\frac{7\\pi}{4}.$$\n\nEach solution is defined in the original equation and gives $1+1+1+2+2=7$.",
